.Dd October 10, 2026
.Dt LIBCAPABILITY 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm capability_get_info ,
.Nm capability_kernel_call ,
.Nm capability_service_connect ,
.Nm capability_service_call ,
.Nm capability_service_call_fds ,
.Nm capability_coalition_watch ,
.Nm capability_coalition_status ,
.Nm capability_coalition_receive ,
.Nm capability_confine_fd
.Nd invoke kernel capability services and observe coalition cleanup
.Sh LIBRARY
Kernel capability wrapper library
.Pq libcapability, Fl lcapability
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In capability.h
.Ft int
.Fn capability_get_info "int fd" "struct capability_info *information"
.Ft int
.Fo capability_kernel_call
.Fa "int fd"
.Fa "const void *request"
.Fa "size_t request_length"
.Fa "const int *request_fds"
.Fa "size_t request_nfds"
.Fa "void *reply"
.Fa "size_t *reply_length"
.Fa "int *reply_fds"
.Fa "size_t *reply_nfds"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn capability_service_connect "int device_fd" "const char *name"
.Ft int
.Fo capability_service_call
.Fa "int fd"
.Fa "const void *request"
.Fa "size_t request_length"
.Fa "void *reply"
.Fa "size_t reply_length"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo capability_service_call_fds
.Fa "int fd"
.Fa "const void *request"
.Fa "size_t request_length"
.Fa "const int *request_fds"
.Fa "size_t request_nfds"
.Fa "void *reply"
.Fa "size_t reply_length"
.Fa "int *reply_fds"
.Fa "size_t expected_reply_nfds"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn capability_confine_fd "int fd"
.Ft int
.Fn capability_coalition_watch "int control_fd"
.Ft int
.Fn capability_coalition_status "int fd" "struct capability_coalition_status *status"
.Ft int
.Fn capability_coalition_receive "int watch_fd" "struct capability_coalition_status *status"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library is the narrow userspace wrapper for synchronous services implemented
by the
.Xr mac_capability 4
kernel subsystem.
It does not discover userspace services, manage supervised processes, frame
asynchronous channel messages, or implement userspace service protocols;
see
.Xr libchannel 3
and
.Xr libservice 3
for those.
.Ss Instance information
.Fn capability_get_info
queries the kernel service instance referenced by
.Fa fd
with
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_GETINFO
and fills
.Fa information .
The caller may set
.Va information->size
to zero or to
.Li sizeof(*information) ;
on return it is
.Li sizeof(*information) .
The structure carries the service
.Va name ,
the instance
.Va badge ,
the per-message
.Va message_limit ,
.Va queue_depth ,
.Va transmit_limit ,
the descriptor limit
.Va max_fds ,
and a
.Va features
bitmask:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct capability_info {
	size_t		size;
	char		name[CAPABILITY_NAME_MAX];
	uint64_t	badge;
	uint32_t	message_limit;
	uint32_t	queue_depth;
	uint32_t	transmit_limit;
	uint32_t	max_fds;
	uint32_t	features;
	uint32_t	reserved[4];
};
.Ed
.Ss Connecting
.Fn capability_service_connect
issues
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_CONNECT
on
.Fa device_fd ,
a descriptor for
.Pa /dev/mac_capability
or another descriptor that carries the connect right, naming the kernel
service
.Fa name
(at most
.Dv CAPABILITY_NAME_MAX
bytes including the terminator, for example
.Dq system ,
.Dq capprotect
or
.Dq coalition ) .
It returns a new service instance descriptor with
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
set.
In the plane the device is claimed exclusively by
.Xr capsule 8 ;
ordinary programs receive already connected instance descriptors instead
and call this function only on a delivered connect-capable descriptor.
.Ss Calling
.Fn capability_kernel_call
invokes the service-specific synchronous kernel ABI
.Pq Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_CALL
on
.Fa fd .
The request buffer and request descriptors are borrowed for the duration of
the call; at most
.Dv CAPABILITY_CALL_MAX_FDS
descriptors may be attached or returned.
On entry,
.Fa *reply_length
and
.Fa *reply_nfds
are the capacities of the reply buffer and descriptor array.
On success they contain the exact returned sizes, and the caller owns each
descriptor in
.Fa reply_fds
in attachment-slot order.
Both capacities may be zero.
.Pp
When the two count pointers are valid, failure resets both returned counts to
zero.
When the supplied descriptor capacity is valid, installed reply
descriptors are closed and the caller's descriptor slots are set to
.Dv -1 ,
including failures detected before the kernel call.
The typed request and reply layouts are defined by the individual kernel
capability service headers under
.In dev/mac_capability/ .
.Pp
.Fn capability_service_call_fds
is the exact-shape form of
.Fn capability_kernel_call :
the reply must be exactly
.Fa reply_length
bytes and exactly
.Fa expected_reply_nfds
descriptors.
Any other shape fails with
.Er EPROTO
after closing every descriptor the service handed back and resetting the
caller's slots to
.Dv -1 .
It is the right call for protocols whose reply size is fixed, such as a
token mint that must return exactly one descriptor.
.Pp
.Fn capability_service_call
is the descriptor-free shorthand for
.Fn capability_service_call_fds
with no request descriptors and no expected reply descriptors.
.Ss Confining descriptors
.Fn capability_confine_fd
freezes a held authority descriptor into the calling process: it applies
.Xr cap_xfer_limit 2
with
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE ,
.Xr cap_clofork_limit 2
with
.Dv CAP_CLOFORK_LOCKED
and
.Xr cap_cloexec_limit 2
with
.Dv CAP_CLOEXEC_LOCKED ,
so the descriptor can no longer be transferred over a socket or channel and
is closed on fork and exec with no way to reopen those paths.
A negative
.Fa fd
is ignored and reported as success, which lets callers confine optional
descriptors unconditionally.
.Ss Coalition cleanup
.Fn capability_coalition_watch
returns an owned, nonblocking cleanup observation descriptor.
Its close-on-exec and close-on-fork flags are installed atomically by CALL.
Older kernels that do not support these flags fail; the helper does not fall
back to installing an inheritable descriptor and changing it afterwards.
The view retains coalition state without keeping its control descriptor open.
Closing or revoking the view cancels observation only.
.Pp
Initialize a
.Vt struct capability_coalition_status
with zeroes, or set its
.Va size
field to its current size.
.Fn capability_coalition_status
queries either a control descriptor or a cleanup view.
.Fn capability_coalition_receive
consumes one terminal notification from a view.
Register EVFILT_READ with kqueue to be notified, then receive;
.Er EAGAIN
means no message is available on the nonblocking view.
Only one terminal notification is emitted per view, including for subscriptions
created after completion.
The receive helper respects the descriptor's current blocking mode.
.Pp
Both functions return zero when they successfully report a state, including
FAILED or RELEASED; the return value alone does not certify successful cleanup.
They leave the output unchanged on error, so callers must check the return
value before using it.
The phase is one of CAPABILITY_COALITION_ACTIVE, _DRAINING, _COMPLETE, _FAILED,
or _RELEASED (each name uses the CAPABILITY_COALITION prefix).
Only COMPLETE means all tracked cleanup actions succeeded.
FAILED reports the first error in cleanup_error; RELEASED means resources were
deliberately detached without confirmed cleanup.
CAPABILITY_COALITION_F_RELEASED may also accompany a failure.
The processes, forks, jails, nested, capabilities, and external fields count
outstanding tracked obligations, not allocation references.
Completion does not certify reclamation of shared/static storage or independent
asynchronous work started by service callbacks.
See
.Xr mac_capability_coalition 4 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn capability_service_connect
and
.Fn capability_coalition_watch
return the new owned descriptor on success and
.Dv -1
on error.
The other functions return zero on success and
.Dv -1
on error, setting
.Va errno .
.Sh ERRORS
In addition to errors from
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr cap_xfer_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_clofork_limit 2
and
.Xr cap_cloexec_limit 2 ,
these functions may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
An argument, buffer, size, or descriptor count is invalid; a descriptor is
negative; a request is empty; a count exceeds
.Dv CAPABILITY_CALL_MAX_FDS ;
or a non-zero capacity was given with a
.Dv NULL
buffer.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The service name passed to
.Fn capability_service_connect
does not fit in
.Dv CAPABILITY_NAME_MAX .
.It Bq Er EPROTO
The kernel returned malformed metadata (an unterminated service name) or
counts exceeding the supplied capacities, or an exact-shape call received a
reply of a different size or descriptor count.
Coalition helpers also reject unrecognized phases, flags, negative errors,
and nonterminal receive notifications.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The temporary ABI object could not be allocated.
.El
.Pp
Service-specific failures are returned unchanged from the kernel service;
see the individual service pages such as
.Xr mac_capability_system 4
and
.Xr mac_capability_capprotect 4 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cap_cloexec_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_clofork_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_xfer_limit 2 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr libchannel 3 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr mac_capability 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability_capprotect 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability_coalition 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability_system 4 ,
.Xr capsule 8
.Sh HISTORY
The kernel-only
.Nm
interface first appeared in 5BSD 1.0.
.Fn capability_service_connect ,
.Fn capability_service_call ,
.Fn capability_service_call_fds
and
.Fn capability_confine_fd
were added in 5BSD 16.0.
